AD  - Dept. of Food Science and Technology, Suez Canal University, Ismailia, 41522, Egypt
AB  - Different packaging procedures were tested for their ability to maintain postharvest quality and to extend the shelf life of 'Early Grand' peach fruit. Fruit were stored in open aluminum trays, unperforated paper bags, perforated paper bags, and perforated polyethylene bags at 2°C and 85-95% relative humidity. No significant weight loss, volume change, or increase in titratable acidity was found (after 30 days of storage) for peaches stored in perforated polyethylene bags. Titratable acidity of stored peaches of other treatments increased while pH decreased over time. Soluble solids content increased most in fruit stored in trays and perforated paper bags. Change in soluble solids was least noticeable for peaches stored in perforated polyethylene bags. Total sugars and total phenolics increased significantly after 30 days of storage. In 1992 and 1993, PPO activity was high at harvest and decreased during storage, then started to increase after 30 days of storage for the unpacked, perforated paper, and paper packed fruit. However, PPO activity significantly decreased in 1992 while increased in 1993 during storage for fruit in polyethylene bags.
